import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';

const introContent = `
    <p>Twenty minutes in a throwaway folder, running the commands and reading what changed after each one. Nothing here gets kept. The point is to watch the files appear and be able to say out loud what each one is.</p>
`;

const step1 = `mkdir npm-practice
cd npm-practice
pwd`;

const step2 = `npm init -y`;

const initOutput = `{
  "name": "npm-practice",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "main": "index.js",
  "scripts": {
    "test": "echo \\"Error: no test specified\\" && exit 1"
  },
  "keywords": [],
  "author": "",
  "license": "ISC"
}`;

const step3 = `npm install --save-dev http-server`;

const afterInstall = `npm-practice/
├── node_modules/          ← new, and very large
├── package.json           ← changed
└── package-lock.json      ← new`;

const step4 = `{
  "scripts": {
    "serve": "http-server -p 3000"
  }
}`;

const step5 = `npm run serve`;

const serveOutput = `Starting up http-server, serving ./

Available on:
  http://127.0.0.1:3000
  http://192.168.1.14:3000
Hit CTRL-C to stop the server`;

const step6 = `{
  "scripts": {
    "list": "ls > files.txt",
    "serve": "http-server -p 3000",
    "start": "npm run list && npm run serve"
  }
}`;

const step7 = `npm run start`;

const cleanup = `cd ..
rm -rf npm-practice`;
---

<PageLayout
  slug="/make-a-package-json/"
  title="Make a package.json and read it out loud"
  subtitle="Twenty minutes, one throwaway folder, every file explained"
  description="A hands-on walk through npm init, installing a dev dependency, writing npm scripts, and chaining commands. Every file that appears is named and explained as it appears."
  avatarImage="https://res.cloudinary.com/cynthia-teeters/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto,w_200,c_limit/canvas/hap/hap-scientist"
  avatarAlt="HAP wearing goggles and holding a bubbling test tube"
  introContent={introContent}
  footerReminder="Reading the file after every command is the habit that makes the rest of this stop being guesswork."
>

  <section class="content-section">
    <div class="topic-section">
      <h2>Before You Start</h2>

      <p>You need a terminal open and Node installed. Typing <code>node --version</code> should print a number rather than an error. On Windows, use Git Bash rather than PowerShell or Command Prompt, because the quoting rules differ and the difference shows up later in a way that is genuinely hard to spot.</p>

      <p class="mt-1">Everything below happens in a folder you are going to delete at the end. Nothing here is a deliverable.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <div class="topic-section">
      <h2>Step 1: Make An Empty Folder And Stand In It</h2>

      <div class="prompt-box">
        <div class="prompt-label">In your terminal:</div>
        <CodeBlock code={step1} lang="bash" />
      </div>

      <p class="mt-1"><code>mkdir</code> makes the folder. <code>cd</code> moves the terminal into it. <code>pwd</code> prints where the terminal currently is, and it should end in <code>npm-practice</code>.</p>

      <p class="mt-1">That last command matters more than it looks. Every npm command acts on the folder the terminal is standing in, and it never asks whether that was the folder you meant. A surprising share of confusing npm behaviour is a command that ran perfectly in the wrong place.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <div class="topic-section">
      <h2>Step 2: Create The package.json</h2>

      <div class="prompt-box">
        <div class="prompt-label">In your terminal:</div>
        <CodeBlock code={step2} lang="bash" />
      </div>

      <p class="mt-1">Without <code>-y</code>, npm asks a series of questions. With it, npm accepts every default and writes the file immediately. Open <code>package.json</code> and read it before going on.</p>

      <div class="prompt-box mt-1">
        <div class="prompt-label">package.json, straight out of npm init:</div>
        <CodeBlock code={initOutput} lang="json" />
      </div>

      <p class="mt-1">Read it out loud, field by field. <code>name</code> came from the folder name. <code>version</code> is a starting point nobody uses for a website. <code>scripts</code> holds one placeholder that does nothing useful. The <a href="/reference/package-json/">package.json reference page</a> goes through every field properly.</p>

      <p class="mt-1">The important observation is what is absent. There is no list of dependencies yet, because nothing has been installed.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <div class="topic-section">
      <h2>Step 3: Install One Tool</h2>

      <div class="prompt-box">
        <div class="prompt-label">In your terminal:</div>
        <CodeBlock code={step3} lang="bash" />
      </div>

      <p class="mt-1"><code>http-server</code> is a small program that serves files over HTTP. The <code>--save-dev</code> flag files it as a development tool rather than something a visitor's browser needs.</p>

      <p class="mt-1">Three things changed on disk.</p>

      <div class="prompt-box mt-1">
        <div class="prompt-label">The folder now:</div>
        <CodeBlock code={afterInstall} lang="text" />
      </div>

      <p class="mt-1">Open <code>package.json</code> again. It now has a <code>devDependencies</code> block naming <code>http-server</code> and a version. Open <code>package-lock.json</code> and scroll. It is thousands of lines describing every package that came down, including the ones <code>http-server</code> depends on that you never named. Look at <code>node_modules</code> and notice how many folders are in there for one small tool.</p>

      <div class="hap-note-callout">
        <img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/cynthia-teeters/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto,w_150,c_limit/canvas/hap/hap-thumbs-up" alt="HAP giving an enthusiastic thumbs up with sparkles around him" width="150" height="150" class="hap-note-image" decoding="async" loading="lazy" />
        <div class="hap-note-content">
          <h3>🟠 A message about vulnerabilities is not a failure</h3>
          <p>npm often ends an install by reporting a number of known vulnerabilities. That is a report about the packages, not an error in what you typed. The install finished. Whether any of those findings matter depends on whether the affected code ever runs in a place that handles untrusted input, which for a local development tool is usually no.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <div class="topic-section">
      <h2>Step 4: Write A Script</h2>

      <p>An installed tool is not on your system path. Typing <code>http-server</code> by itself will most likely report that the command was not found. npm scripts exist to solve exactly this: inside a script, npm looks in <code>node_modules</code> first.</p>

      <p class="mt-1">Open <code>package.json</code>, find the <code>scripts</code> block, and replace it with this.</p>

      <div class="prompt-box mt-1">
        <div class="prompt-label">package.json, the scripts block:</div>
        <CodeBlock code={step4} lang="json" />
      </div>

      <p class="mt-1">Save the file. Forgetting to save produces <code>Missing script: "serve"</code> on the next command, which is the single most common thing to go wrong in this whole exercise.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <div class="topic-section">
      <h2>Step 5: Run It</h2>

      <div class="prompt-box">
        <div class="prompt-label">In your terminal:</div>
        <CodeBlock code={step5} lang="bash" />
      </div>

      <div class="prompt-box mt-1">
        <div class="prompt-label">Roughly what comes back:</div>
        <CodeBlock code={serveOutput} lang="text" />
      </div>

      <p class="mt-1">The terminal stops and sits there. That is correct and it is new. Most commands run, print, and give you your prompt back. A server does not finish, because finishing would mean it stopped serving. It holds the terminal until you stop it.</p>

      <p class="mt-1">Open <code>http://127.0.0.1:3000</code> in a browser. There is no HTML file in this folder, so you get a file listing. That listing is being generated and delivered by a program running on your own machine. Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> and <kbd>C</kbd> in the terminal to stop it, and the prompt comes back.</p>

      <p class="mt-1">The <a href="/why-a-dev-server/">page on dev servers</a> covers what the port number is doing and why any of this is necessary.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <div class="topic-section">
      <h2>Step 6: Chain Two Commands</h2>

      <p>A script can run other scripts, and commands can be strung together. Replace the scripts block again.</p>

      <div class="prompt-box mt-1">
        <div class="prompt-label">package.json, the scripts block:</div>
        <CodeBlock code={step6} lang="json" />
      </div>

      <div class="prompt-box mt-1">
        <div class="prompt-label">In your terminal:</div>
        <CodeBlock code={step7} lang="bash" />
      </div>

      <p class="mt-1">Two characters in there are doing real work and neither is JavaScript.</p>

      <div class="analysis-grid analysis-grid--two-col">
        <div class="analysis-point">
          <h3>The greater-than sign</h3>
          <p><code>ls &gt; files.txt</code> takes what <code>ls</code> would have printed to the screen and writes it into a file instead. This is called redirection. It overwrites the file every time. Two of them in a row, <code>&gt;&gt;</code>, appends rather than overwrites.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="analysis-point">
          <h3>The double ampersand</h3>
          <p><code>a &amp;&amp; b</code> means run <code>a</code>, and run <code>b</code> only if <code>a</code> succeeded. Success is measured by the exit code, a number every command returns when it finishes, where zero means fine. Chains stop at the first failure rather than plowing ahead on broken input.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-1">Stop the server, then open <code>files.txt</code>. It holds the folder listing, written there by the first half of the chain before the second half started. Turning a multi-step job into one command a teammate can run without instructions is the actual point of the scripts block.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <div class="topic-section">
      <h2>Step 7: Throw It Away</h2>

      <div class="prompt-box">
        <div class="prompt-label">In your terminal:</div>
        <CodeBlock code={cleanup} lang="bash" />
      </div>

      <p class="mt-1">Deleting it is part of the exercise. Everything in that folder except <code>package.json</code> can be regenerated from <code>package.json</code>, and being willing to delete <code>node_modules</code> without a second thought is a genuinely useful reflex.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <div class="topic-section">
      <h2>What To Take Away</h2>

      <div class="tips-container">
        <div class="tip-card">
          <div class="tip-number">1</div>
          <div>
            <h3>The terminal's current folder decides everything</h3>
            <p>npm acts on wherever the terminal is standing. <code>pwd</code> before an install costs one second and prevents a real class of confusion.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
          <div class="tip-number">2</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Read package.json after every command that touches it</h3>
            <p>The file is short and it is the record of what you did. Watching it change is how the commands stop being incantations.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
          <div class="tip-number">3</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Save before you run</h3>
            <p><code>Missing script</code> nearly always means the editor still holds unsaved changes.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
          <div class="tip-number">4</div>
          <div>
            <h3>A server holding the terminal is correct behaviour</h3>
            <p>It has not hung. Open a second terminal tab if you need one, and stop the server with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> and <kbd>C</kbd> when you are done.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
          <div class="tip-number">5</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Scripts are shell, not JavaScript</h3>
            <p>The contents of a script line are handed to your shell. <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> are shell features, which is why the choice of terminal on Windows matters.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</PageLayout>

